d_at_ms\":1788251960251}}\n/home/ubuntu/.codex/sessions/2026/08/31/rollout-2026-08-31T06-03-01-01a05669-fc4c-7aa0-b9a1-034d833ed8ed.jsonl:14928:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-01T08:39:20.255Z\",\"ordinal\":14927,\"type\":\"response_item\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"custom_tool_call_output\",\"id\":\"ctco_01a05c1f-73bf-7b31-858d-4f0d1121ae2a\",\"call_id\":\"call_bvnnjr2TjmP8biUoRZLlJi9g\",\"output\":[{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"Script completed\\nWall time 0.1 seconds\\nOutput:\\n\"},{\"type\":\"input_text\",\"text\":\"/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md.20260901_prefill_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/CD3_CURRENT_MANIFEST.md.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/apply_cd3.sh\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/apply_cd3.sh.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_cpu_weight_kv_opt.patch\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_cpu_weight_kv_opt.patch.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_cpu_weight_kv_opt.sha256\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_cpu_weight_kv_opt.sha256.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.patch\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.patch.20260901_prefill_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.patch.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.sha256\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.sha256.20260901_prefill_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/cd3_current.sha256.20260901_route_before.bak\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/serving_max_batched_tokens.patch\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/serving_max_batched_tokens_from_1024.patch\\n/lambda/nfs/whchoi/startup-patches/tiered-serve/serving_performance_suite.patch\\n# CD3 startup patch backup\\n\\n- Generated: 2026-09-01 UTC\\n- Source repository: `/root/projects/tiered-serve`\\n- Source HEAD: `5d56a972bb80444671b9d27293e3fa18076c0d58`\\n- Consolidated patch SHA-256: `95ad90192897b34d11f79f0cbc541552a61346cacd60d99b01bfdeb2fecf0c92`\\n- Files changed: 27\\n\\n## Startup order\\n\\n1. `serving_max_batched_tokens.patch` (or its `from_1024` variant)\\n2. `serving_performance_suite.patch`\\n3. `apply_cd3.sh /root/projects/tiered-serve`\\n\\n`apply_cd3.sh` verifies and applies the consolidated `cd3_current.patch`.\\nIt is idempotent. The old two-stage patch files are retained only as rollback\\nartifacts and are not loaded at container startup.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nThis snapshot contains the current CD3 weight, fused-GEMM, KV codec, CPU\\nweight/KV encoders, benchmark, serving integration, model configuration, and\\nCD3 tests. Weight encode defaults to CPU; CUDA is used only to stage completed\\ncompressed buffers and for decode/fused-MM. The legacy GPU encoder is available\\nonly through the explicit debug override `TIERED_SERVE_CD3_WEIGHT_CPU_ENCODE=0`.\\nUnrelated working-tree changes were intentionally excluded.\\n\\nThe CPU KV encoder additionally contains the 2026-09-01 optimization set:\\n\\n- block-owner, single-source-pass encoding;\\n- min/max exception-free fast path;\\n- SVE2 predicate counting on the exception path;\\n- NEON hierarchical packing of 16 three-bit codes;\\n- uninitialized temporary exception scratch.\\n\\nDirect-to-persistent Packed20 packing was measured but not retained because it\\nwas 3–6% slower for medium and large batches.\\n\\nThe consolidated snapshot additionally contains:\\n\\n- host-native CD3 weight encoding with 64-row stripe parallelism;\\n- adaptive OpenMP team sizing for a single KV compression worker;\\n- batched NumPy KV slot allocation instead of a per-block Python loop;\\n- production fused-MM crossover calibration through N=128 on GH200.\\n- 128-thread standalone CD3 decode, with two logical chunks per thread;\\n- two half-width live output accumulators with the same two 128-bit stores,\\n  reducing standalone decode latency without changing the format;\\n- 2-D `(tile, block)` KV decode launch geometry;\\n- direct-to-cache indexed KV decode with no raw-BF16 intermediate copy;\\n- selected-run KV staging for partial restores below the slot high-water mark.\\n- one aligned slot-major CD3 blob and row-stride-aware CPU/GPU kernels;\\n- one `cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` submission for all K/V staging runs;\\n- cached block/slot device metadata and one native K/V decode dispatch.\\n- active-set identity/content caching with generation-safe invalidation;\\n- a dense steady-state decode plan that skips filtering, sorting and run scans.\\n- a fully CPU P4Flat weight post-process (blocked relayout and subtile codec),\\n  so CPU weight encoding no longer launches CUDA encode kernels;\\n- a frozen-split weight benchmark that separately reports H2D, resident GPU\\n  decode, and their combined latency.\\n- projection-specific fused-N calibration overrides, with a uniform override\\n  retained for compatibility;\\n- exact-length CD3 frozen-split staging views, so standalone fallback accepts\\n  variable-sized Packed20x3 exception streams;\\n- startup route reporting that reflects projection-specific overrides.\\n- a GH200/Llama-70B prefill weight-stream cutoff of N=1856: compressed\\n  standalone decode below the cutoff and raw BF16 transfer at or above it;\\n- an AST-level regression test that pins the calibrated CD3 cutoff.\\n\\n## Validation\\n\\n- The old CD3 patch's complete path set is contained in this patch.\\n- Applied cleanly after startup prerequisites on source HEAD.\\n- All 27 restored files matched the selected live files byte-for-byte.\\n- `git diff --check`, Python byte compilation, and shell syntax checks passed.\\n- The consolidated patch applied after both startup prerequisites in a clean\\n  worktree and all 27 restored files matched the live files byte-for-byte.\\n- 33 CD3, prefill weight-stream, and KV-prefill policy tests passed.\\n- Actual Llama-3.1-70B weight round-trip: qkv/o/gateup/down all bit-exact.\\n- Layer-40 CD3 CPU weight encode median total: 130.7 ms, versus P4Flat CPU\\n  1951.9 ms (about 14.9x faster); compression ratio about 1.368x versus 1.326x.\\n- GH200 CPU KV encode median with one serving worker, K+V: N=8 0.16 ms;\\n  N=64 0.27 ms; N=128 0.37 ms; N=256 0.47 ms; N=1024 0.99 ms;\\n  N=2048 2.02 ms. All cases were bit-exact.\\n- Full serving initialization installed all 320 fused Linear overrides and\\n  completed all 320 CPU-compressed weight cells.\\n- Layer-40 standalone weight decode median gained another 1.4% in aggregate\\n  from half-width live output accumulation (all four groups bit-exact).\\n- Resident production KV decode improved 1.69x--2.04x for N=8/64/256 by\\n  writing directly to the final cache. Partial H2D restore at high-water=1024\\n  improved 16% for N=64 and 12% for N=256.\\n- CPU-encoded production KV H2D+decode steady-state median on GH200 is\\n  0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for N=8/64/256/1024. P4Flat under the same\\n  split-measurement run is 0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms. CD3 resident decode is\\n  0.028/0.029/0.036/0.065 ms versus P4Flat 0.046/0.047/0.049/0.082 ms; CD3\\n  H2D is 0.016/0.026/0.056/0.173 ms versus 0.030/0.042/0.081/0.232 ms.\\n  The pre-blob CD3 values were\\n  0.247/0.291/0.374/0.762 ms; all cases were bit-exact. This historical P4Flat\\n  comparison used its raw-safe 32,768-byte blob stride. Current P4Flat defaults\\n  to a 25,600-byte tight stride, so these P4Flat values must not be used as the\\n  final current-codec comparison.\\n- Layer-40 frozen-split weight medians on GH200 (qkv/o/gateup/down summed):\\n  CD3 H2D 1.200 ms, resident decode 0.923 ms, H2D+decode 2.127 ms; P4Flat\\n  H2D 1.353 ms, resident decode 0.906 ms, H2D+decode 2.232 ms. CD3 therefore\\n  transfers 11.3% faster and finishes the production pair 4.7% faster while\\n  its isolated decode is 1.9% slower. All four weights were bit-exact.\\n- Rejected experiments are not present: warp-owner exception pre-application\\n  was about 2.3x slower, pairwise 3xU32 code loading was neutral/regressive,\\n  64-thread or register-forced 10-CTA kernels were 7--12% slower, and a\\n  separate standalone exception kernel was 50.7% slower than integrated patch.\\n- Fused route calibration with concurrent H2D found all four projections faster\\n  for every tested N=65..128. At N=129 the Npad=192 transition makes fused\\n  about 1.6--1.7x slower, so the production cutoff is exactly 128.\\n- An all-80-layer QKV sweep found fused wins on 79/80 layers and saves\\n  0.508/0.595/1.211 ms in aggregate at N=80/96/128. The exception-heavy layer\\n  0 is the sole loser. End-to-end TPOT P50 for all-fused versus QKV-standalone\\n  was 171.90/188.40/219.26 versus 172.20/187.96/219.30 ms at batch\\n  80/96/128, confirming a tie at engine level and favoring all-fused because it\\n  avoids dense-weight HBM writes.\\n- End-to-end forced-route prefill sweeps placed the standalone/raw crossover\\n  between N=1824 and N=1856. At N=1824 standalone led by 18.630 ms; at N=1856\\n  raw led by 20.405 ms, and raw remained ahead through N=3072. An unforced run\\n  confirmed standalone routing at N=1792/1824 and raw routing at N=1856/1888.\\n  The full report and source logs are stored under\\n  `/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/`.\\n#!/usr/bin/env bash\\nset -euo pipefail\\n\\nrepo_path=\\\"${1:-/root/projects/tiered-serve}\\\"\\npatch_dir=/opt/tiered-serve-startup-patches\\nbase_patch_path=\\\"$patch_dir/cd3_current.patch\\\"\\n\\n(cd \\\"$patch_dir\\\" && sha256sum --check cd3_current.sha256)\\n\\nif git -C \\\"$repo_path\\\" apply --reverse --check \\\"$base_patch_path\\\" >/dev/null 2>&1; then\\n    echo \\\"[CD3] consolidated startup patch already applied\\\"\\n    exit 0\\nfi\\n\\ngit -C \\\"$repo_path\\\" apply --check \\\"$base_patch_path\\\"\\ngit -C \\\"$repo_path\\\" apply \\\"$base_patch_path\\\"\\necho \\\"[CD3] consolidated startup patch applied\\\"\\n\"}],\"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough\":{\"turn_id\":\"01a05c04-6b2b-77a2-9c2d-e99009e95651\",\"create_time\":1788251960.2554467}}}\n/home/ubuntu/.codex/sessions/2026/08/31/rollout-2026-08-31T06-03-01-01a05669-fc4c-7aa0-b9a1-034d833ed8ed.jsonl:14957:{\"timestamp\":\"2026-09-01T08:39:53.114Z\",\"ordinal\":14956,\"type\":\"event_msg\",\"payload\":{\"type\":\"item_completed\",\"thread_id\":\"01a05669-fc4c-7aa0-b9a1-034d833ed8ed\",\"turn_id\":\"01a05c04-6b2b-77a2-9c2d-e99009e95651\",\"item\":{\"type\":\"CommandExecution\",\"id\":\"exec-68fcd0c6-80d2-4219-9c79-5a8fa329d8d2\",\"process_id\":\"2235\",\"command\":[\"/bin/bash\",\"-lc\",\"sed -n '1,520p' /lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\"],\"cwd\":\"file:///home/ubuntu\",\"parsed_cmd\":[{\"type\":\"read\",\"cmd\":\"sed -n '1,520p' /lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\",\"name\":\"CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\",\"path\":\"/lambda/nfs/whchoi/experiment-results/cd3-20260901-route/CD3_OPTIMIZATION_AND_P4FLAT_COMPARISON.md\"}],\"source\":\"unified_exec_startup\",\"status\":\"completed\",\"stdout\":\"# CD3 optimization and P4Flat comparison\\n\\nDate: 2026-09-01 UTC\\n\\n## 1. Executive conclusion\\n\\nThe final CD3 implementation is not uniformly faster than P4Flat in every\\nisolated operation. Its advantage comes from combining a denser lossless\\nformat with substantially faster CPU encoding, less LPDDR-to-HBM traffic, and\\na Hopper WGMMA fused path tuned for the decode batch sizes used by the serving\\nworkload.\\n\\nOn the tested GH200 and Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct configuration:\\n\\n- Total compressed weight size is about 3.1% smaller: CD3 ratio is about\\n  1.368x versus P4Flat 1.326x.\\n- The LPDDR-resident portion transferred per layer in frozen-split mode is\\n  376.586 MiB for CD3 versus 414.502 MiB for P4Flat, 9.15% fewer bytes.\\n- CPU weight encoding of one layer's four fused projections is 14.9x to 28.1x\\n  faster across the recorded runs. The latest median-of-five run was\\n  128.830 ms versus 3620.297 ms.\\n- Steady-state CPU KV encoding is 1.20x to 2.88x faster over N=8..2048.\\n- Weight standalone H2D+decode is 4.36% faster overall. The H2D part is 10.89%\\n  faster, while isolated resident decode is 1.45% slower.\\n- In the latency-critical fused route, the sum of the four layer-40 projection\\n  medians is 7.08% faster than P4Flat at N=64 and 14.62% faster at N=128 when\\n  each codec uses its production route.\\n- KV H2D-only and resident-decode-only are consistently faster in CD3. In the\\n  current one-stream standalone `decompress_batch` benchmark, however, P4Flat's\\n  lower dispatch/preparation overhead makes its total 6--12% faster at\\n  N=8/64/256; N=1024 is tied. A previously reported CD3 total win used\\n  P4Flat's legacy 32,768-byte blob stride, not its current 25,600-byte default.\\n\\nThere is not yet a clean final-revision, apples-to-apples full serving\\nTTFT/TPOT run of final CD3 against final P4Flat. The percentages above are\\ncomponent and per-layer route measurements. Older 2026-08-27 serving results\\npredate the final CD3 kernels and routing, so they are intentionally excluded\\nfrom the headline comparison.\\n\\n## 2. Test platform and terminology\\n\\n- GPU/system: NVIDIA GH200, compute capability 9.0, Grace ARM CPU, CUDA 13.2.\\n- Model weights: real `meta-llama/Llama-3.1-70B-Instruct`, TP=1, BF16.\\n- Representative layer: transformer layer 40 unless otherwise stated.\\n- Fused serving weights:\\n  - QKV: 10240 x 8192, 160 MiB raw BF16.\\n  - O: 8192 x 8192, 128 MiB.\\n  - GateUp: 57344 x 8192, 896 MiB.\\n  - Down: 8192 x 28672, 448 MiB.\\n  - Total per layer: 1632 MiB (1.59375 GiB).\\n- `N` is the flattened activation row/token count presented to a linear layer.\\n- All retained codec paths are lossless; reported comparisons passed raw BF16\\n  bit-exact checks.\\n\\nThe measurements use the following distinct definitions:\\n\\n- CPU encode: BF16 input is on the CPU. Time includes production codec CPU\\n  work and synchronization, but never a CUDA encode kernel.\\n- Resident decode: compressed data is already in GPU HBM. Only the GPU decode\\n  kernel is timed.\\n- H2D-only: only the LPDDR/pinned-host buffers required for the selected path\\n  are copied into preallocated HBM staging. On GH200 this is Grace LPDDR to GPU\\n  HBM over NVLink-C2C, not a conventional discrete-GPU PCIe transfer.\\n- H2D+decode: the production transfer and decode operation is timed. It is an\\n  independent measurement, not necessarily the arithmetic sum of separately\\n  measured H2D and decode medians.\\n- Fused: compressed weight reconstruction occurs in the register-A WGMMA\\n  kernel and no dense BF16 weight is emitted to HBM.\\n- Standalone: the full BF16 weight is reconstructed in HBM and then consumed\\n  by a separate GEMM.\\n- Raw: the full uncompressed BF16 weight is transferred and no codec decode\\n  kernel runs.\\n\\n## 3. Format and memory footprint\\n\\nCD3 preserves the BF16 sign and mantissa byte verbatim and encodes the exponent\\nas a 3-bit delta from one base exponent per 16 values. A 64x64 tile therefore\\ncontains 1536 code bytes, 256 base bytes, and a sparse exception stream.\\nExceptions use a 20-bit `(12-bit tile position, 8-bit exponent)` entry, packed\\nthree entries into each 64-bit word (`Packed20x3`).\\n\\nCodes 0 through 7 are all normal CD3 deltas. An exception temporarily carries\\ncode 0 and is distinguished by its Packed20 position/mask. Consequently code 7\\ncannot be used as an exception sentinel without changing the format. The\\nEscape7 experiment increased exceptions by 85.6%, slowed decode by 11.11%, and\\nwould reduce the expected KV compression ratio to about 1.3347x, close to\\nP4Flat's 1.3259x.\\n\\nFor representative layer-40 weights:\\n\\n| Metric | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| Total compression ratio | 1.368x | 1.326x | 3.1% fewer compressed bytes |\\n| Frozen-split LPDDR transfer/layer | 376.586 MiB | 414.502 MiB | 9.15% fewer bytes |\\n\\nBoth codecs retain sign/mantissa in HBM for frozen-split weights. CD3 also\\nretains tile exception offsets in HBM. The table includes all transferred\\nmetadata, so auxiliary structures are accounted for in the comparison.\\n\\nKV is different: offloaded KV does not retain the sign/mantissa half in HBM.\\nThe selected K and V compressed slot blobs are transferred in full and decoded\\ndirectly into the final GPU KV cache.\\n\\n## 4. CPU encoding\\n\\n### 4.1 Weight CPU encode\\n\\nInput was the four real layer-40 fused BF16 CPU tensors listed above. Each\\ncodec ran in a fresh process on NUMA node 0, with one untimed warm-up and five\\ntimed repetitions per projection; the table contains medians. No GPU encoder\\nwas called.\\n\\n| Projection | CD3 (ms) | P4Flat (ms) | Speedup |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| QKV | 15.824 | 354.944 | 22.4x |\\n| O | 12.710 | 285.589 | 22.5x |\\n| GateUp | 79.161 | 1987.135 | 25.1x |\\n| Down | 21.135 | 992.629 | 47.0x |\\n| Sum of medians | 128.830 | 3620.297 | 28.1x |\\n\\nAn earlier recorded run was 130.7 ms versus 1951.9 ms (14.9x). CD3 is stable\\naround 129--131 ms, while P4Flat CPU time is sensitive to host/OpenMP state.\\nThe defensible conclusion is an order-of-magnitude win, observed between\\n14.9x and 28.1x, rather than one universal multiplier.\\n\\nWeight encode is normally an offline/load-time step, so this gain mostly\\nreduces initialization/preprocessing time and prevents codec preparation from\\nstealing GPU SM cycles; it is not added to token latency.\\n\\n### 4.2 KV CPU encode\\n\\nInput was real cached K and V blocks from\\n`/root/projects/ats-benchmarks/benchmarks/compress/kv_blocks_cache.pt`, each\\nblock shaped `[16,8,128]` BF16. K+V is 64 KiB per block. The production\\n`compress_batch_async_from_host` plus `wait_compress_async` path was measured\\nwith one compression worker, `OMP=all`, NUMA node 0, one warm-up and 20 timed\\niterations. GPU decode was used only after timing to verify bit exactness.\\n\\n| Blocks N | K+V input | CD3 median | P4Flat median | CD3 speedup |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 8 | 0.5 MiB | 0.17 ms | 0.49 ms | 2.88x |\\n| 64 | 4 MiB | 0.28 ms | 0.55 ms | 1.96x |\\n| 128 | 8 MiB | 0.40 ms | 0.60 ms | 1.50x |\\n| 256 | 16 MiB | 0.49 ms | 0.68 ms | 1.39x |\\n| 1024 | 64 MiB | 1.04 ms | 1.25 ms | 1.20x |\\n| 2048 | 128 MiB | 1.99 ms | 2.48 ms | 1.25x |\\n\\n## 5. Weight standalone H2D/decode comparison\\n\\nThis used `benchmarks/weight_cpu_h2d_decode.py` with the real layer-40 fused\\nweights, CPU encoding for both codecs, 10 warm-ups and 50 samples. Encode and\\nsetup were outside CUDA timing. Sign/mantissa stayed in HBM; only the\\nLPDDR-side exponent representation was transferred using the same\\n`cudaMemcpyBatchAsync` helper for both codecs.\\n\\n| Sum across QKV/O/GateUp/Down | CD3 | P4Flat | CD3 change |\\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\\n| H2D-only | 1.2027 ms | 1.3497 ms | 10.89% faster |\\n| Resident decode-only | 0.9211 ms | 0.9079 ms | 1.45% slower |\\n| Serialized H2D+decode | 2.1286 ms | 2.2256 ms | 4.36% faster |\\n\\nThus the weight win is a transfer win, not a pure decoder win. CD3's 3-bit\\ncodes reduce the LPDDR traffic enough to compensate for a slightly more\\nexpensive decoder. Per-projection H2D+decode medians were also all lower for\\nCD3: QKV 0.2183 vs 0.2338 ms, O 0.1808 vs 0.1904 ms, GateUp 1.1430 vs\\n1.1920 ms, and Down 0.5864 vs 0.6095 ms.\\n\\n## 6. KV H2D/decode comparison\\n\\nThe input was the same real K/V block cache as the CPU encode test. Each codec\\nand block count ran in a new process with CPU encoding, one worker, 20 warm-ups\\nand 100 CUDA-event samples. Three complete rounds were run; the table reports\\nthe median of the three per-process medians. No GPU encoder was called.\\n\\nThis benchmark constructs a compressor directly and never constructs a\\n`DualMemoryLoader`; consequently `load_kv_stream=None` and `decompress_batch`\\nuses its standalone one-stream fallback. It is a codec/dispatch microbenchmark,\\nnot a full serving-pipeline measurement.\\n\\n| N | CD3 H2D | P4 H2D | CD3 resident decode | P4 resident decode | CD3 standalone total | P4 standalone total |\\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\\n| 8 | 0.0171 | 0.0289 | 0.0309 | 0.0458 | 0.0703 | 0.0629 |\\n| 64 | 0.0260 | 0.0376 | 0.0287 | 0.0448 | 0.0686 | 0.0647 |\\n| 256 | 0.0559 | 0.0666 | 0.0371 | 0.0466 | 0.1004 | 0.0915 |\\n| 1024 | 0.1730 | 0.1849 | 0.0668 | 0.0736 | 0.2486 | 0.2497 |\\n\\nAll values are milliseconds. CD3 reduces isolated H2D by 40.8%, 30.9%, 16.1%,\\nand 6.4%, and isolated resident decode by 32.5%, 35.9%, 20.4%, and 9.2% at\\nthe four sizes. The complete one-stream call is 11.8%, 6.0%, and 9.7% slower\\nat N=8/64/256 and tied at N=1024. P4Flat's C++ `decode_layer_prep` has lower\\nfixed preparation/dispatch cost; at small N this outweighs CD3's faster copy\\nand kernel in the complete-call timing.\\n\\nThe earlier result remembered as N=8 tied and CD3 faster above N=8 was\\n0.065/0.065/0.102/0.251 ms for CD3 versus\\n0.063/0.070/0.107/0.302 ms for P4Flat. It used P4Flat's legacy raw-safe\\n`TIERED_SERVE_KV_BLOB_STRIDE=0`, which gives a 32,768-byte row. Current P4Flat\\ndefaults to a tight 25,600-byte row. Re-running legacy stride at N=1024\\nreproduced H2D/decode/total = 0.229/0.081/0.300 ms, matching the old\\n0.232/0.082/0.302 ms. Thus that ordering was a real result for the old P4Flat\\nconfiguration, but it is not the correct comparison against current P4Flat.\\n\\n## 7. Fused decode+GEMM comparison\\n\\nInput weights were the four real layer-40 projections. Activations were seeded\\nrandom BF16 `[N,K]` tensors. Sign/mantissa stayed resident in HBM 